.docname {Releasing}
.include {docs}

Releases are built from tags. CI builds Linux, Windows, and macOS and publishes them as a GitHub release.

## Bump the version

Set the new version in `Cargo.toml`, then build once so `Cargo.lock` picks it up, and commit both:

```shell
cargo build
git commit -am "release 0.2.0"
```

CI refuses a tag that doesn't match this version, so the tag, the release, and the crate always name the same version. Check the tag before pushing it:

```shell
just release-check v0.2.0
```

This runs `scripts/check-release-version.sh`, which passes only if the tag is `v` followed by the version in `Cargo.toml`, and `Cargo.lock` has the same version.

## Tag the release

```shell
git tag v0.2.0 && git push origin v0.2.0
```

On a tag, CI runs the usual lint and test jobs, then:

| Job               | Output                                         | Notes                                    |
|-------------------|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| `version`         | Nothing                                        | Runs the same check as `just release-check`; no release job starts unless it passes |
| `release-linux`   | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-linux.tar.gz`           | Built on Debian trixie, so it needs glibc 2.39 or newer |
| `release-windows` | `gol-bevy-<tag>-x86_64-windows.zip`            | Cross-compiled with MinGW, so no Windows runner is needed |
| `release-macos`   | `gol-bevy-<tag>-universal-macos.tar.gz`        | Universal binary for Apple silicon and Intel, built on a macOS runner |
| `release`         | A GitHub release named `gol-bevy <tag>`        | Has all three archives attached          |

## Release notes

The release notes are generated by GitHub: a line on how to run the download, then the pull requests merged since the previous release, their authors, and a link to the full list of commits. Changes pushed straight to `main` appear only in that list of commits, so land anything worth mentioning through a pull request with a clear title. Edit the notes on the release page afterwards to add anything more.

Release binaries are built with `--locked` and stripped of symbols, which are about a third of the binary. Local release builds keep them for profiling.

## Building for macOS locally

`release-macos` runs the same recipe CI uses. On a Mac, run it to try a release build:

```shell
just release-macos v0.2.0
```

This adds both Apple targets, builds for Apple silicon and Intel, joins them into a universal binary with `lipo`, and writes `dist/gol-bevy-v0.2.0-universal-macos.tar.gz`. Without a version, it uses `git describe --tags --always`.

## Archive layout

`scripts/package-release.sh` packages every platform the same way:

```shell
scripts/package-release.sh <archive name> <binary> [tar.gz|zip]
```

It copies the binary, `README.md`, and `LICENSE` into a folder named after the archive, packs it into `dist/`, and prints the archive path.

.filetree
    - dist
      - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux.tar.gz
        - gol-bevy-v0.2.0-x86_64-linux
          - gol-bevy
          - README.md
          - LICENSE
